# The clock.
#
# Environmental, and the one prelude primitive that is not about a
# representation the language cannot see. "When is now?" is answered by the
# same declaration that answers "where does this live?":
#
#   state renderedAt is static Whole from clock    # frozen at deploy
#   state now        is client Whole from clock    # read at mount
#
# The placement is where the freshness decision is written down, and there
# is nowhere else to write it. A clock read inside a function body would
# produce a derived signal that never recomputes despite its value being
# stale — the silent-staleness failure §5.5 cites Svelte's runes post
# about — which is why §17.4.9 confines one to a signal initialiser.
#
# THE ONE IMPURE PRIMITIVE, and the reason `gives pure T` exists at all
# (§21.9). Sixteen of the seventeen carry the marker. This one must not:
# it takes no arguments and returns a different value on every call, so it
# is the canonical impure foreign, and the two lines above are the standard
# library *demonstrating* it returning different values at different
# placements.
#
# Note that `is anywhere` above is still correct and still says nothing
# about this. It answers which bundles the clock may be linked into, and
# the honest answer is both — a browser has a clock and so does a server.
# Reading that word as a purity claim was residual risk R1, and it made a
# clock read `⨆ ∅ = Trusted` forever. Without the marker its result is
# Untrusted, and a `release` body that reaches it is refused by REL-PURE.

foreign clock is anywhere
    from "zd:time" as "now"
    gives Whole

# --- the civil calendar --------------------------------------------------
#
# A *moment* is what `clock` gives: a `Whole` of milliseconds since
# 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z. Everything below reads one apart or puts one back
# together, and every function here is O(1), pure, and adds no primitive.
#
# WHAT UNBLOCKED THIS. §17.4.10's local binding, and nothing else. The
# algorithm below names nine intermediates, and until a body had somewhere
# to keep one each of the nine had to become a top-level `of`-function
# recomputing all of its predecessors: on the order of a hundred `floor`
# calls for one date where the same algorithm in C++ performs nine. That
# cost, and not a missing primitive, is why #118 recorded the date layer as
# blocked on #15. `civilDateOf` performs ten: Hinnant's nine, plus the one
# that turns milliseconds into a day number.
#
# ⚠️ UTC, AND ONLY UTC. There is no local time here and no time zone
# argument. A zone is a table that changes several times a year by
# political decision, so it is neither a rule the language could state nor
# a fact a compiled bundle could hold and stay correct. It is also the one
# input that would make `static` non-deterministic: §17.4.8 requires a
# build to produce the same answer everywhere, and a date rendered at build
# time in the builder's zone would not. A program that needs a visitor's
# local time has the offset available where the visitor is, and can add it
# to a moment before reading it apart. That is an offset in milliseconds,
# which is arithmetic the language already has.
#
# ⚠️ NO FORMATTER. `text of` prints a number and `join` puts pieces
# together, so a program writes the format it wants out of the fields
# below. A library one would have to pick a locale, and picking one is the
# same table problem as the zone with an ordering convention on top.
#
# WHY A RECORD RATHER THAN `yearOf`, `monthOf`, `dayOf`. Three functions
# would be three separate ten-`floor` walks to answer one question, which
# is a third of the defect this file exists to close. One walk returns all
# three fields, and `(civilDateOf of moment).year` is the year.

record CivilDate
    year  is Whole
    month is Whole
    day   is Whole

record CivilTime
    hour        is Whole
    minute      is Whole
    second      is Whole
    millisecond is Whole

# ⚠️ EVERY DIVISION BELOW IS WRITTEN `valueOr … fallback is 0`, AND THE
# FALLBACK IS UNREACHABLE. `quotient` and `mod` give an `Option` because a
# zero divisor has no answer (see `number.zd`). Every divisor here is a
# non-zero literal, so `None` cannot occur, and the elimination is written
# out at each site rather than hidden behind a helper. A helper would be a
# second spelling of `quotient` that invents an answer for zero, which is
# exactly what `option.zd` says the library must not do on the caller's
# behalf.

# The day a moment falls in: days since 1970-01-01, UTC. O(1).
#
# Floored, not truncated, so the day before the epoch is -1 rather than 0
# and the number is usable as a coordinate. The difference of two of these
# is a count of days, which is how a duration in days is written.
function dayOf of moment
    give valueOr with maybe is (quotient with value is moment, divisor is 86400000), fallback is 0

# The civil date a moment falls on, UTC. O(1), ten `floor` calls.
#
# Howard Hinnant's `civil_from_days`, which is exact over the whole range a
# `Whole` can hold and needs no table and no leap-year branch. It works by
# shifting the year to start in March, which puts the leap day last and
# makes the month lengths a repeating pattern that one division reproduces.
# `era` is a 400-year cycle, `doe` the day within it, `yoe` the year within
# it, `doy` the day within the shifted year, and `mp` the shifted month.
#
# `zdc-codegen/tests/library.rs` asserts both the answers and the count.
function civilDateOf of moment
    with z is (dayOf of moment) + 719468
    with era is (valueOr with maybe is (quotient with value is z, divisor is 146097), fallback is 0)
    with doe is z - (era * 146097)
    with skipped is (valueOr with maybe is (quotient with value is doe, divisor is 1460), fallback is 0) - (valueOr with maybe is (quotient with value is doe, divisor is 36524), fallback is 0) + (valueOr with maybe is (quotient with value is doe, divisor is 146096), fallback is 0)
    with yoe is (valueOr with maybe is (quotient with value is doe - skipped, divisor is 365), fallback is 0)
    with doy is doe - ((365 * yoe) + (valueOr with maybe is (quotient with value is yoe, divisor is 4), fallback is 0) - (valueOr with maybe is (quotient with value is yoe, divisor is 100), fallback is 0))
    with mp is (valueOr with maybe is (quotient with value is (5 * doy) + 2, divisor is 153), fallback is 0)
    with dayInMonth is doy - (valueOr with maybe is (quotient with value is (153 * mp) + 2, divisor is 5), fallback is 0) + 1
    with shiftedYear is yoe + (era * 400)
    if mp < 10
        give CivilDate with year is shiftedYear, month is mp + 3, day is dayInMonth
    give CivilDate with year is shiftedYear + 1, month is mp - 9, day is dayInMonth

# The time of day a moment falls at, UTC. O(1), four `floor` calls.
#
# Each field is a division of the whole of `sinceMidnight` rather than of
# the remainder the field above left, so no two of them can disagree about
# where the boundary was.
function civilTimeOf of moment
    with sinceMidnight is moment - ((dayOf of moment) * 86400000)
    with hours is (valueOr with maybe is (quotient with value is sinceMidnight, divisor is 3600000), fallback is 0)
    with minutes is (valueOr with maybe is (quotient with value is sinceMidnight, divisor is 60000), fallback is 0)
    with seconds is (valueOr with maybe is (quotient with value is sinceMidnight, divisor is 1000), fallback is 0)
    give CivilTime with hour is hours, minute is minutes - (hours * 60), second is seconds - (minutes * 60), millisecond is sinceMidnight - (seconds * 1000)

# The day of the week a moment falls on, UTC: 0 is Sunday, 6 is Saturday.
# O(1), two `floor` calls.
#
# 1970-01-01 was a Thursday, which is where the 4 comes from. `mod` is the
# floored remainder, so this is correct before the epoch too, where
# JavaScript's own `%` would give a negative weekday.
function weekdayOf of moment
    give valueOr with maybe is (mod with value is (dayOf of moment) + 4, divisor is 7), fallback is 0

# The moment a civil date and time name, UTC. O(1), six `floor` calls.
#
# Hinnant's `days_from_civil`, and the exact inverse of `civilDateOf` and
# `civilTimeOf` together: `library.rs` asserts the round trip in both
# directions rather than asserting each half against a table.
#
# The March shift is arithmetic rather than a branch here, because both
# halves of it are needed and a division computes them together: for a
# month before March, `quotient` of `month - 3` by 12 is -1 and `mod` is 10
# or 11, which is the borrow and the shifted month at once.
#
# ⚠️ AN OUT-OF-RANGE FIELD IS NOT REFUSED, IT IS CARRIED. A 13th month is
# the January after, and a 32nd of January is the 1st of February, which is
# the property that makes `momentOf` usable for date arithmetic: add a
# month by adding 1 to `month` and read the answer back apart.
function momentOf with date, time
    with monthIndex is (valueOr with maybe is (mod with value is date.month - 3, divisor is 12), fallback is 0)
    with shiftedYear is date.year + (valueOr with maybe is (quotient with value is date.month - 3, divisor is 12), fallback is 0)
    with era is (valueOr with maybe is (quotient with value is shiftedYear, divisor is 400), fallback is 0)
    with yoe is shiftedYear - (era * 400)
    with doy is (valueOr with maybe is (quotient with value is (153 * monthIndex) + 2, divisor is 5), fallback is 0) + date.day - 1
    with doe is (yoe * 365) + (valueOr with maybe is (quotient with value is yoe, divisor is 4), fallback is 0) - (valueOr with maybe is (quotient with value is yoe, divisor is 100), fallback is 0) + doy
    with days is (era * 146097) + doe - 719468
    give (days * 86400000) + (time.hour * 3600000) + (time.minute * 60000) + (time.second * 1000) + time.millisecond
